'''''Dypsis''''' is a genus of [[flowering plant]]s in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Arecaceae]]. They are slender, [[evergreen]] palms with yellow flowers carried in [[panicles]] amongst the [[pinnate]] leaves. Many ''Dypsis'' species have aerial branching (above the main trunk), a rare [[growth habit]] among palms.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Johnson |first1=Dennis V. |chapter=Introduction |title=Tropical palms. 2010 revision |date=2010 |publisher=Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO) |location=Rome |series=FAO Technical Papers |page=2 |url=https://www.fao.org/4/i1590e/i1590e00.htm |ISBN=978-92-5-106742-0 }}</ref> Some have [[marcescent]] leaves that remain attached after death and trap litter for nutrients.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Bramwell|first1=David|last2=Caujapé-Castells|first2=Juli|title=The Biology of Island Floras|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2fCYWrKJAu4C&pg=PA189|date=2011-07-21|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=978-1-139-49780-0|page=189}}</ref>  Several species previously placed here have been returned to the restored genera ''[[Chrysalidocarpus]]'' (including the [[type species]] ''[[Chrysalidocarpus lutescens]]'') and ''[[Vonitra]]''.

== Etymology ==
The [[etymology]] is obscure but may be related to the Greek ''{{Transliteration|grc|dypto}}'' 'I dive' or ''{{Transliteration|grc|dyptes}}'' 'diver'.<ref>{{cite book|last=Quattrocchi|first=Umberto|title=CRC World Dictionary of Plant Names|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=esMPU5DHEGgC&pg=PA45|year=1999|publisher=CRC Press|isbn=978-0-8493-2675-2|page=45}}</ref> The species are native to [[Tanzania]], [[Madagascar]], and various islands in the [[Indian Ocean]] ([[Mauritius]] and [[Comoros]]). A few are naturalized in other regions, especially in the [[Caribbean]].<ref name=wcsp>[http://apps.kew.org/wcsp/namedetail.do?name_id=65391 Kew World Checklist of Selected Plant Families]</ref>

